6.130.m:
And reciting it in the presence of Viṣṇu with this disposition of intellect, / one enjoys happiness in this world and attains the supreme state.

This king of continents was established on earth forever. / Count them by number, O lord, having shown mercy toward me.

Mahādeva said: / All-pervading, he is to be seen everywhere on earth among all beings. / Whatever is seen in the seven worlds, with moving and unmoving beings,

that reality, O goddess, has not been seen or heard by me without him. / Therefore Mahādeva, Keśava, the destroyer of distress, is Viṣṇu; / he exists in the form of sacred bathing-places on this continent, O ruler of the gods.

Now I shall declare those sacred bathing-places; there is no doubt about this. / The first is the sacred place Puṣkara, the eminent, auspicious among sacred bathing-places; / the second is Vārāṇasī, the sacred place granting liberation;

the third is the sacred place Naimiṣa, traditionally remembered as purifying for sages; / and the fourth is Prayāga, traditionally remembered as the best of sacred bathing-places;

the fifth is declared to be Kārmuka, arisen on Gandhamādana; / the eleventh is Kānyakubja, where Vāmana dwells;

the seventh is Viśvakāya, on the auspicious lower mountain; / the eighth is named Gautama, created of old on Mandara;

the ninth is Madotkaṭa; the tenth is Rathacaitraka; / the eleventh is Kānyakubja, where Vāmana dwells;

the twelfth is Malaya; and then Kubjāmraka; / Viśveśvara, Girikarṇa, and Kedāra, which bestows the [supreme] state;

Bāhya on the back of the Himavat; Gopaka in Gokarṇa; / Sthāneśvara in the Himālaya; Bilvapatrika in Bilvaka;

on Śrīśaila, the sacred place Mādhava; Bhadra likewise in Bhadreśvara; / Vijaya is declared in Vārāha; the Vaiṣṇava [sacred place] on the Vaiṣṇava mountain;

the Raudra in Rudrakoṭi; the Paitrya on mount Kāliñjara; / the Kāpila sacred place in Kapila; and Karkoṭaka in Mukuṭa;

the sacred place arisen from Śālagrāma is in the Gallikā [river], O ruler of the gods; / that called Śiva is in the Narmadā; Viśvarūpaka in Māyā;

Utpalākṣa and Sahasrākṣa arose on mount Raivata; / in the Gaṅgā is the Pitṛtīrtha, and that arisen from Viṣṇu's foot;

Vipāpā on the Vipāśā; Puṇḍravardhana in Pāṭala; / Nārāyaṇa on Supārśva; Viṣṇu's temple on Trikūṭa;

on Vipula, [the sacred place] named Vipula; Kalyāṇa on mount Malaya; / Kaurava at Koṭitīrtha; Sugandha on Gandhamādana;

Trisandhya at Kubjāmraka; Haripriya at Gaṅgādvāra; / Śaila in the Vindhya region; that known as Sārasvata at Badarī;

Kālarūpa on the Kālindī; Sāyaka on the Sahya; / Cāndra in the Candra region; Ramaṇa, the foremost of sacred bathing-places;

that named Mṛga on the Yamunā; Kurūdbhava in Karavīra; / and the sacred place named Umā on Mount Vināyaka;

Ārogya in the Bhāskara country; Maheśvara in Mahākāla; / the sacred place named Abhayada; that named Amṛta in the Vindhya pass;

Viśvarūpa in Maṇḍapa; that named Svāhā in the city of Īśvara; / Vaigaleya in Pracaṇḍā; Cāṇḍika in Marakaṇṭaka;

Someśvara in the sacred bathing-place; Puṣkara in Prabhāsa; / Devamātṛ on the Sarasvatī, situated on the bank of the Pārāvata;

Mahālaya in Mahāpadma; Piṅgaleśvara on the Payoṣṇī; / likewise the sacred place in Siṃhikā; that named Ravi in Saurabha;

Kārtika in Kṛttikākṣetra; Śāṅkara on mount Śaṅkara; / then the divine [sacred place] named Utpala at the confluence of Subhadrā and the sea;

then Gāṇapatya on the mountain named Viṣṇu; / then that sacred place declared in Jālandhara which is Viṣṇumukha;

Tāraka declared in Tāra on the mountain named Viṣṇu; / Pauṇḍra in Devadāruvana; Pauṣka in the Kaśmīra region;

Bhauma and Hima in the Himālaya; and again Tuṣṭika and Pauṣṭika; / and the sacred place Kapālamocana arisen in Māyāpura;

and then Śaṅkhoddhāra, [where there is] the god holding a conch; / in Piṇḍa, that named Piṇḍa; in Siddha, Vaikhānasa;

Viṣṇukāma on Acchoda, bestowing dharma, desire, wealth, and liberation; / Auṣadhya on the northern bank; Kuśodaka in Kuśadvīpa;

Manmatha on Hemakūṭa; Satyavādana on Kumuda; / the sacred place Vāśvaka in Vadaṃtī; Mātṛka in the Vindhya;

the sacred place composed of brahman within the mind is traditionally remembered as purifying among sacred bathing-places. / Hear the best of all these sacred bathing-places, O beautiful lady:

There was never, nor will there ever be, a sacred bathing-place consisting of Viṣṇu's name. / A killer of a brahmin, a stealer of gold, a killer of a child, or a killer of a cow

is freed merely by the name, through the grace of Keśava. / In the Kali age, charming Dvāravatī is blessed, [and] blessed is the god Janārdana.

Those men who see the god, their liberation is completely certain. / Thus I meditate, O great goddess, on the most blessed god,

bathing and other [rites] in this [river] become a hundred times greater than at Kurukṣetra. / At the confluence of the Vārtaghnī, O goddess, headed by Gaṇeśvara,

an assembly of sacred places was formerly brought into the Sābhramatī by the Gaṇas. / This confluence of sacred places has been declared by me in brief.

Even the lord of speech is unable [to state] the detailed truth of the sacred places. / Truth is a sacred place, compassion is a sacred place, restraint of the senses is a sacred place.

One should diligently bathe in her sacred place, without any doubt. / The morning is three muhūrtas, and the morning-period is of the same duration;

then bathing and other rites in the holy place give pleasure to the gods. / Midday consists of three muhūrtas, and the afternoon comes after that;

bathing, rice-balls, and water-libations [then] bring joy to the ancestors. / Evening consists of three muhūrtas; one should not perform śrāddha then.

That time is called rākṣasī, despised for all sacred acts. / Fifteen muhūrtas of the day are always known;

therein the eighth muhūrta is remembered as the time called kutupa. / Because at midday the sun always slows down,

therefore, from the offering of rice-balls to the ancestors, it gives infinite fruit. / Midday, a rhinoceros-horn vessel, and a Nepalese blanket,
